WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.240
- There's Chicago P.D.,
there's Chicago Fire,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.990
but I'm most concerned
with, you guessed it,

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.170
Chicago Med, let's get started.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.420
(lively hip-hop music)

00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:16.600
(train tracks)

00:00:18.759 --> 00:00:19.707
- Doesn't look too bad, all right?

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:21.420
You're gonna be all right.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.313
- Good triage.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.920
- 45 year old male left leg amputation.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.753
- Treatment two.

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:28.950
- Trey Moderman, GCS
three BP of 90 by palp.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.330
- GCS three is the Glasgow Coma Scale,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.430
three is very bad you
need to be intubated,

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.130
you're basically in a
life or death situation

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.110
at that moment.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.990
- He keeps biting down and
I can't get the blade in.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.120
- Pressure

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.470
- You bag him, I'll do CPR.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.220
- If you can't insert a
tube to help someone breathe

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.980
the one thing you can do is
you can continue bagging them.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.050
That still oxygenates their airway,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.000
it's obviously not as ideal as
putting an endotracheal tube

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.300
in but the most important thing is CPR.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.610
Chest compressions, chest
compressions, chest compressions.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.300
- Almost there it's not time yet.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.750
(grunting)

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.680
- Never ideal to do chest
compressions with one arm

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.260
like that but I can
understand if he's been doing

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.750
chest compressions for 15
minutes, it's really tough

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.390
it's tiring to do chest compressions.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.050
I've done chest compressions
for a while and generally

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.767
after each round you have
a student or a resident

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:23.850
standing behind you ready to jump in

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.450
and start doing the chest compressions.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.970
Cause it's all about at least getting

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.520
two inches of compressions
to the chest which can

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.560
sometimes break ribs, but
it's all about getting them

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.298
enough time to get to the hospital

00:01:34.298 --> 00:01:35.630
to hopefully save their life.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.540
- I got it from here man.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.700
- Nope, he's mine.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.970
- Okay who the hell is this guy?

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:39.847
- [Nurse] He says he's a doc.

00:01:39.847 --> 00:01:41.670
- All right get off the gurney, Doc.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.010
I'm senior resident in this ER.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.510
- Yeah, well I'm your new trauma fellow.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.940
- Doesn't happen like that.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.700
Trauma fellows don't just
run in, this guy could be

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.920
anybody you need to see some ID.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.990
I'm all for the show trying
to set up the stage for

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.720
this character, but
people don't just run into

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.350
the hospital and say
"I'm the doctor now!".

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.550
- [Doctor] Get a BP keep giving him blood.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.324
Send a rainbow of tubes
and and a type and cross.

00:02:02.324 --> 00:02:04.660
Get his blood gas and get him to the OR.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.830
- Type and cross again you
try and find out the correct

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.580
blood type for now
getting O-negative is fine

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.920
but you don't wanna keep
wasting the O-negative blood.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.380
So you wanna do a type and cross.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.320
When he says the rainbow
of tubes that's basically

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.940
tubes that are sent off for
a wide variety of tests--

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.880
a wide array of tests like
a CBC which is a complete

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.960
blood count, BMP or CMP
which is a metabolic panel,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.953
a PT/INR which shows clotting
abilities and clotting times.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.253
- No gag reflex didn't even
need sedation, not good.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.083
- We got another problem,
this woman's pregnant.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.970
- What he's doing there with
the ultrasound is called

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.530
a FAST scan, it basically
allows you to see if there's

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.905
any internal bleeding going
on especially in the abdomen

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.580
cause you can lose a lot
of blood in the abdomen

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.320
without even knowing
that you're bleeding into

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.153
the abdomen.

00:02:57.153 --> 00:02:59.990
But obviously the situation
he's found that this patient

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:03.030
is pregnant which complicates
a situation even more.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.170
- 20 year old male, cystic
fibrosis, complaining of chills,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.066
labored breathing, temp's 98.

00:03:08.066 --> 00:03:10.407
- Jamie, that you?

00:03:10.407 --> 00:03:12.060
- Hi Maggie.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.626
- Back so soon?

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:14.459
- I missed you guys.

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:18.880
- Cystic fibrosis is a-- it's
a deadly illness unfortunately

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.900
and it basically you build up
so much mucus in your airway

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.950
because you cannot clear the
mucus out of your airways

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.680
efficiently as someone
who is healthy and doesn't

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.140
have cystic fibrosis.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.029
So you get very often lung
infections, obstruction in

00:03:34.029 --> 00:03:38.110
your airway and most people
live somewhere into their

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.950
40's or 50's with the illness
and that's pretty much a

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.780
life expectancy with someone
who has cystic fibrosis.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.770
- Mr. Delson I see that you're
using inhaled antibiotics,

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.500
Tobramycin, Albuterol. You wear
a vibration jacket at night

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.683
and use a humidifier?

00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:53.516
- Yeah.

00:03:53.516 --> 00:03:55.610
- The vibration jacket actually
helps break up some of that

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.410
mucus and allows it to
naturally come out when that

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.243
patient coughs.

00:03:59.243 --> 00:04:00.850
Very commonly we use something known as

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.260
Chest Physical Therapy,
which is almost like clapping

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.800
on the chest to help
clear some of the mucus.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.000
Parents of young children
who have cystic fibrosis

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.270
are trained in giving their
children this procedure.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.331
- Baby checks out fine.

00:04:11.331 --> 00:04:12.740
(relieved gasp)

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.993
Healthy heartbeat, amniotic
sac and placenta are intact.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.400
Gina though, I'm afraid,
suffered a traumatic head injury

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.900
and so far she's unresponsive.

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:29.140
We need to contact her family,
if you have any information.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.140
- My husband and I are
pretty much her family she--

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.660
she's been living with us since before

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.230
the embryo was implanted.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.524
- That's such a difficult
situation, I mean to have

00:04:37.524 --> 00:04:41.570
someone be a surrogate to carry your baby

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.030
and then they are living
with you and they're involved

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.410
in this horrible accident and they have

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.550
a traumatic brain injury
where they're being

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.100
kept alive by machines.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.480
That's gonna be a tough
decision whether or not to keep

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.680
her alive using machines so
that the baby can continue

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.198
growing and it get to at
least to a stage where

00:04:57.198 --> 00:05:01.920
the baby can survive on it's
own following a C section.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.753
Man!

00:05:02.753 --> 00:05:04.823
- Jamie I've got your test results back.

00:05:06.089 --> 00:05:09.070
I wish they were a
little more encouraging.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.630
You're pancytopenic which means

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.610
that your cell counts are low.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.114
You've got a blood Ph of 7.14.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.620
- When your blood Ph is
that low that means you're

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.104
trapping carbon dioxide within your body

00:05:20.104 --> 00:05:23.260
and you're having
obstruction which comes from

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.530
the cystic fibrosis.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.610
That's very dangerous
because when you go into

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.050
an acidotic state like
that your enzymes think

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.560
your body can't function and you can die.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.130
- I'd like to intubate
you, put you to sleep,

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:35.520
get you on a ventilator.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.501
You're getting fatigued, this
would just allow you to rest

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:40.100
so that the antibiotics can do their job.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:45.100
- I've been intubated eight
times, I think I'll skip it.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.440
- The purpose of intubating
someone who has such

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:54.900
a strong acidosis is
when you put the tube in

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.430
it allows the person to not
work to breathe so heavy

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.350
to expel all that carbon dioxide.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.440
The machine basically does it for them,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.730
and again it allows them to
rest, it allows the antibiotics

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.720
to do their job and it
improves the Ph quicker than

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.080
the person can do themselves.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.380
Very uncomfortable, not a
lot of patients like it.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.800
I've never seen-- and again
I'm not a trauma surgeon,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.530
I'm not an ER doctor but
I haven't seen options

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.570
given that frankly to patients.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:25.570
If a Ph was that low in my
hospital generally we would

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:29.220
strongly advice the patient
to intubate them, but again

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.726
I'm not super specialized in this field so

00:06:32.726 --> 00:06:36.042
I'm just going off based
off my own experiences.

00:06:36.042 --> 00:06:38.209
(beeping)

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:41.643
- Sherri?

00:06:41.643 --> 00:06:42.720
- [Sherri] Dr. Halstead.

00:06:44.223 --> 00:06:45.290
- You need to blow down her CO2,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.600
take her off the vent and bag her,

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.580
give her a dose of Mannitol
and increase her dopamine

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:49.413
drip to 12.

00:06:49.413 --> 00:06:50.560
- Right away.
- Doctor?

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.830
- Gina's intercranial pressure is rising.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.940
- What happens after an
injury to the brain is

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.400
just like any part of the body.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.280
There exist inflammation,
swelling, edema and the

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.490
problem with having swelling
inside the cranium is that

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.710
the cranium is a skull, it's
meant to keep things from

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.620
coming in and protect you
but it also crams the brain

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.130
when there's edema and swelling
and it actually damages

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.030
the brain further and can be fatal.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.920
So, you try and control it
with medications, you try

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.950
and give the patient something
to reduce the swelling

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.677
in the brain, there's also some
physical things you can do.

00:07:25.677 --> 00:07:29.470
Like elevating the head to
hopefully get some of the

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.000
fluid to come down.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.284
But, in reality if the
condition gets worse

00:07:33.284 --> 00:07:37.670
you need to do the surgery to
open a piece of the cranium

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.076
to allow the brain to swell and
not cause increased pressure

00:07:41.076 --> 00:07:42.570
within the brain.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.340
- Intubating Jamie is the
only hope we have of keeping

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.173
him alive.

00:07:47.173 --> 00:07:48.006
- What's that gonna get him?

00:07:48.006 --> 00:07:48.839
A week?

00:07:48.839 --> 00:07:49.672
10 days?

00:07:49.672 --> 00:07:52.300
- It could be six months, a year, more.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.043
- This goes to the heart of
the issue of patient autonomy.

00:07:55.043 --> 00:07:57.100
That they have the right to choose

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.450
what happens to their body.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:02.160
I don't know how old Jamie is,
once you're at the age of 18

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.040
or older you have the right to
turn down medical treatments,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:09.120
you have the right and say
that as long as you understand

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.430
the consequences of what
you're doing, you can undergo

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.390
or skip any treatment that you want.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.290
Nobody can force treatments upon you.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.363
I understand what the ER
doctor's doing, I also understand

00:08:19.363 --> 00:08:21.030
what the psychiatrist is doing.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.723
- As soon as we hear
something we'll let you know.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.233
Rachel?

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:28.050
Rachel can you hear me?

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:28.920
- What's wrong?

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:30.305
What's happening?

00:08:30.305 --> 00:08:32.130
- I can't find a pulse.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:32.963
- Oh my god.

00:08:34.427 --> 00:08:36.215
(alarm blares)

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:38.950
- [Woman over PA] Treatment three.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.260
- Well the medical student is
doing the exact right thing.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:44.810
Call the alert, start doing
chest compressions right away.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:45.960
- You're not compressing the heart Sarah,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.180
you've gotta go harder.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.283
- But she's so small I don't wanna--

00:08:48.283 --> 00:08:49.653
- Harder, do it, let's go!

00:08:51.287 --> 00:08:52.530
(ribs cracking)

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:53.793
Come on Sarah, harder!

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.390
Hold!

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.223
(beeping)

00:08:59.223 --> 00:09:01.590
There we go back in sinus, get a BP.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:02.560
- What happened?

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.160
- She arrested, we got her back.

00:09:05.109 --> 00:09:06.412
- Can you get an IV?

00:09:06.412 --> 00:09:07.470
- Hand me a drill, get an IO line.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.950
- An IO line is an intraosseous line.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.580
In children sometimes it's
difficult to put an IV line

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:16.440
sometimes they need extra
fluids, what we can do is drill

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.300
a little needle right-- it
sounds brutal but it's really

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.080
not that bad, drill a little
needle directly into the

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.710
shinbone and what that allows us to do

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:27.310
is to give extra fluids and
medications to that area.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.060
- I just broke a little girls ribs.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.600
- Reese, you saved the girls life.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.090
- Breaking someones ribs
while doing proper CPR

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:39.310
is par of the course and the
CPR actually saves lives,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.170
the ribs are an unintended
consequence but they're a

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:44.510
side effect of trying to
keep the young girl alive.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.250
- This is Jorge's?
(speaks spanish)

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.083
- Yes.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:52.333
- Inside, I found this.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.090
- Why would you give that
to her in this moment?

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:03.810
That's so unnecessary!

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.000
I promise this will not
happen in the hospital.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.663
- What happened with the little girl?

00:10:07.663 --> 00:10:10.060
- She had a cardiac contusion,

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:11.330
bruising caused her to arrest.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.810
She's good now, but it was a real scare.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.280
- Sometimes when you have a
direct injury to the heart

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.207
that can cause a a contusion
which is just a bruise,

00:10:18.207 --> 00:10:20.770
and sometimes that can mess
with the electrical activity

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.790
of your heart and cause
it-- cause you to have a

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.340
cardiac arrest which means that it just

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:26.700
stops the cardiac rhythm.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.276
Actually this is a common
scenario during baseball games

00:10:29.276 --> 00:10:32.670
if someone hits a line
drive and it hits someone

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.240
in the infield directly in
the chest, if it hits in the

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.950
specific time of the electrical
rhythm you can actually

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:38.860
stop the heart.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:42.323
- Jorge's brain was without
oxygen for too long.

00:10:44.438 --> 00:10:47.355
(speaking spanish)

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.720
- We have translators in the
hospital for moments like this.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.790
If you're gonna have a
conversation that's about life

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:56.350
or death you need a translator there.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.400
Your broken Spanish isn't the right way

00:10:58.400 --> 00:10:59.650
to communicate with a patient.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:04.650
- He's 20 years old, without
new lungs he will die.

00:11:05.227 --> 00:11:08.310
- (speaking spanish)

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:11.933
- Your fiance,

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:14.731
the man you love--

00:11:14.731 --> 00:11:16.640
- (speaking spanish)

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:18.983
- The man who loved you,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:23.090
can save his life.

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:25.240
- A little unethical here,
they're talking about the

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.670
patient's condition to
a non family member,

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.230
it's technically breaking
information protocol,

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:31.660
but I don't think they'd
get in trouble for this

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.380
I think they're doing the right thing

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:37.123
and that's why a lot of rules
are open to interpretation

00:11:37.123 --> 00:11:40.210
and I think here they're
looking out for the best

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:44.280
interest of the patient and
maybe telling his patient

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:47.130
information to somebody else
but in hopes to save his life.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.620
So I think legally this isn't
right but I think morally

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.140
and ethically it's the right move.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.175
- Going back to the hospital?

00:11:52.175 --> 00:11:53.008
- Yeah.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.203
Wouldn't mind being
there when he wakes up.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:11:59.473
- Can I come?

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:03.863
- Sure.

00:12:03.863 --> 00:12:07.280
- I appreciate that the doctors
wanna spend their off time

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.520
with a patient but you
know, you gotta recharge

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:11.353
your own batteries.

00:12:11.353 --> 00:12:13.590
You gotta get the seven
to nine hours of sleep so

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.540
you can make the best
decisions for your patients

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:16.600
the following day.

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.630
Again, you wanna be
empathetic but you don't wanna

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.970
burn out either because then
your future patients will

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.800
suffer also so it's a
fine line between being a

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.893
good empathetic doctor
and burning yourself out.

00:12:26.893 --> 00:12:29.643
(dramatic music)

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:36.380
This is a good show, quality
actors, quality script,

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:39.870
good medical info, showed
some very unique situations

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.700
that have inherent drama built into them,

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:43.920
but they were realistic.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.600
Based off this pilot
episode I'm definitely a fan

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:48.310
so thank you for recommending this.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:49.820
